                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p><strong>Physical Chemistry</strong>&nbsp;is a compulsory course for undergraduate students majoring in Pharmacy. It systematically covers core topics such as chemical thermodynamics, chemical kinetics, electrochemistry, surface chemistry, and colloidal dispersion systems, while exploring the application of physicochemical principles in pharmaceutical sciences. Through a combination of theoretical instruction and laboratory experiments, the course aims to equip students with the ability to analyze and solve practical problems in drug development, production, and quality control using physicochemical methods. It also lays a theoretical foundation for subsequent specialized courses such as Pharmaceutics and Pharmaceutical Analysis.</p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
